---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import { DebtChart } from '../../components/islands/DebtChart'
import { AttributionSplit } from '../../components/islands/AttributionSplit'
import { debt, budget, vintageOf } from '../../data'

const HAND_WRITTEN = new Set(['forty-trillion', 'passed-signed'])

const sections = [
  { id: 'forty-trillion', label: 'The $40 trillion' },
  { id: 'who-holds-it', label: 'Who holds it' },
  { id: 'how-old', label: 'How old is the debt' },
  { id: 'whole-budget', label: 'The whole budget' },
  { id: 'structural-gap', label: 'The structural gap' },
  { id: 'what-congress-votes-on', label: 'What Congress votes on' },
  { id: 'net-interest', label: 'Net interest' },
  { id: 'the-laws', label: 'The laws' },
  { id: 'passed-signed', label: 'Who passed it, who signed it' },
  { id: 'where-money-comes-from', label: 'Where the money comes from' },
  { id: 'by-state', label: 'By state' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What this cannot tell you' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Government | US federal finance"
  description="Federal taxation, spending, debt and the votes behind them, FY1962 to FY2025."
  sections={sections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 3 of 3</p>
  <h1>Government</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    What the federal government collects, what it spends, what it owes, and who voted for
    each of it.
  </p>
  <section id="forty-trillion">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>Ten years, twice the debt</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The US crossed $40 trillion in August 2026. Ten fiscal years ago it was $19.6
      trillion. Measured against the economy, though, the same decade moved the debt from
      105% of GDP to 124%.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57T at the close of FY2016 to $40T in
      August 2026, a 2.04x rise.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57 trillion at the close of FY2016 to $40 trillion in August 2026."
      title="Total public debt outstanding at fiscal year end, 1995 to 2026"
      source={debt._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(debt._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="gross debt in nominal $ trillions, or percent of GDP"
      note="The final point is a daily close, not a fiscal year-end value, and carries no share of GDP because FY2026 GDP is not final."
    >
      <DebtChart client:visible rows={debt.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Doubling is not new. FY2005 to FY2015 ran $7.9 trillion to $18.2 trillion, a 2.3x
      rise, faster than this decade. What changed is the base. That decade added $10.2
      trillion. This one added $20.3 trillion, an average of $2.0 trillion a year.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      CBO projected in May 2023 that the debt would reach $40 trillion in 2028. It arrived
      about two years early.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="passed-signed">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 9</span></p>
    <h2>The same $16.75 trillion, two ways</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Every major law scored since 1995 nets to $16.75 trillion in ten-year costs. Sorted by
      the coalition that voted for it, and by the president who signed it. Both total the same
      number.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      By coalition: cross-party +$9.24T (16 laws), party-line Republican +$5.21T (3),
      party-line Democratic +$2.31T (4). By signing president: Trump I +$6.08T (5), Obama
      +$3.98T (6), Trump II +$3.40T (1), G.W. Bush +$2.79T (4), Biden +$0.50T (5).
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Two summary columns, each totalling $16.75 trillion in net ten-year legislative costs. The left column breaks the total down by the voting coalition that passed each law; the right column by the president who signed it."
      title="Net ten-year legislative cost by voting coalition and by signing president, laws enacted 1995 to 2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="$ trillions, ten-year score at enactment"
      yUnit="voting coalition, or signing president"
      note="Clinton's two 1997 laws are excluded: both predate the ten-year scoring convention and carry no comparable figure. The cross-party coalition passed 16 laws; 14 carry a score, and both counts are shown. Bars and totals are net figures, increases less reductions; gross increases alone total $20.73 trillion."
    >
      <AttributionSplit client:visible />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      These are net figures, increases less reductions. Gross increases alone come to $20.73
      trillion, and on that basis Obama reads $6.22T rather than $3.98T because he also signed
      $2.24 trillion of scored deficit reduction. If you show both, label both.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      A president signs what Congress sends. Coloring by control credits or blames one party
      for laws the other party largely voted for: the 2018 and 2019 budget acts passed under
      unified Republican control but cleared the Senate 71-28 and 67-28.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.filter((s) => !HAND_WRITTEN.has(s.id)).map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}
</BaseLayout>
